    "engine": "jupyter",
    "markdown": "---\ngd-head-tags:\n  - >-\n    `<script type=\"application/ld+json\">[{\"@context\": \"https://schema.org\",\n    \"@type\": \"SoftwareSourceCode\", \"name\": \"EdgeCase\", \"description\":\n    \"High-performance graph analysis library\", \"codeRepository\":\n    \"https://github.com/rich-iannone/edgecase\", \"license\":\n    \"https://opensource.org/licenses/MIT\", \"url\":\n    \"https://rich-iannone.github.io/edgecase\", \"documentation\":\n    \"https://rich-iannone.github.io/edgecase\", \"programmingLanguage\": {\"@type\":\n    \"ComputerLanguage\", \"name\": \"Python\"}}, {\"@context\": \"https://schema.org\",\n    \"@type\": \"WebPage\", \"name\": \"Graph.layout_planar - EdgeCase\", \"isPartOf\":\n    {\"@type\": \"WebSite\", \"name\": \"EdgeCase\"}, \"url\":\n    \"https://rich-iannone.github.io/edgecase/reference/Graph.layout_planar.html\"}]</script>`{=html}\nopen-graph:\n  title: >-\n    [Graph.layout_planar()]{.doc-object-name .doc-function .doc-label\n    .doc-label-method} | EdgeCase\n  description: Place the nodes of a planar graph so that no edges cross.\ntwitter-card:\n  title: >-\n    [Graph.layout_planar()]{.doc-object-name .doc-function .doc-label\n    .doc-label-method} | EdgeCase\n  description: Place the nodes of a planar graph so that no edges cross.\ntitle: >-\n  [Graph.layout_planar()]{.doc-object-name .doc-function .doc-label\n  .doc-label-method}\nbody-classes: doc-api-page doc-py-api-page\nshift-heading-level-by: 0\nbread-crumbs: false\ntoc-depth: 3\ndescription-meta: Place the nodes of a planar graph so that no edges cross.\npage-navigation: false\nhtml-table-processing: none\n---\n\n::: {.doc-subject}\n\nPlace the nodes of a planar graph so that no edges cross.\n:::\n\n::: {.doc-usage-source}\nUsage\n\n[Source](https://github.com/rich-iannone/edgecase/blob/main/python/edgecase/graph.py#L6111-L6217){target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\"}\n:::\n\n::: {.doc-signature .doc-function}\n```pythonsignature\nGraph.layout_planar(\n    refine=True,\n    scale=1.0,\n    center=(0.0, 0.0),\n    attr_name=None,\n    return_dict=False,\n)\n```\n:::\n\n::: {.doc-text}\n\n![Planar layout](../assets/graphs/layout-planar.light.svg){.lightbox fig-alt=\"On the left, the octahedron graph (six nodes, each joined to four others) scattered at random, with several edges crossing. On the right, layout_planar() draws it with no crossings: a large outer triangle of nodes 1, 2 and 4 with the other three nodes inside it.\" width=\"100%\"}\n\nFinds a planar embedding (see `check_planarity()`), adds edges to make it 2-connected\nand to triangulate every face but the largest, which becomes the outer face, and then\nplaces the nodes one at a time in a canonical order with the shift method of Chrobak and\nPayne. Every node lands on an integer grid point, `x` from `0` to `2n - 4` and `y` from\n`0` to `n - 2`, and the edges drawn as straight lines never cross. This ports NetworkX's\n`planar_layout()`, but that drawing crowds the nodes: the triangulation pulls them into a\nbroad triangle, and nodes deep inside it can nearly touch. So with `refine=True`\n(default), a force-directed pass then spreads them out, as in `layout_spring()`: nodes\nrepel each other and the edges near them, and edges pull their ends together. Any move\nthat would make two edges cross, or bring a node onto an edge, is cut short, so the\ndrawing stays crossing-free. The result is centered and scaled so the largest\ncoordinate is `scale=`.\n\nA planar graph has many embeddings and this one need not be the one NetworkX finds, so\neven with `refine=False` the positions can differ from NetworkX's while being just as\ncrossing-free. The refinement keeps every node inside its face, so a node in a small\nface stays cramped, and the drawing suits checking or showing that a graph is planar\nmore than reading it. For a readable picture, a force-directed layout such as\n`layout_spring()` often draws a planar graph without crossings too. The refinement runs\n300 rounds, each costing `O((n + m)^2)` for `n` nodes and `m` edges, with fewer rounds\non larger graphs so that it stays within about a second; from a few thousand nodes it\nis skipped.\n\nEdge direction, self-loops and parallel edges are ignored. Graphs with fewer than four\nnodes use fixed points of a triangle, `(0, 0)`, `(2, 0)` and `(1, 1)`, in node ID order,\nand separate components are joined before triangulating, so they are laid out together.\n\nPositions are stored as `[x, y]` lists in the `attr_name=` node attribute; see\n`layout_circular()`.\n:::\n\n## Parameters {.doc-parameters}\n\n::: {.doc-definition-items}\n<code>[refine]{.doc-parameter-name}[:]{.doc-parameter-annotation-sep} [bool]{.doc-parameter-annotation} [=]{.doc-parameter-default-sep .op} [[True]{.va}]{.doc-parameter-default}</code>\n\n:   If `True` (default), spread the nodes out without letting any edges cross. If\n    `False`, keep the integer grid drawing of Chrobak and Payne, as NetworkX does.\n\n<code>[scale]{.doc-parameter-name}[:]{.doc-parameter-annotation-sep} [Optional[float]]{.doc-parameter-annotation} [=]{.doc-parameter-default-sep .op} [[1.0]{.fl}]{.doc-parameter-default}</code>\n\n:   Largest coordinate after rescaling. `None` keeps the raw coordinates, on the integer\n    grid when `refine=False` (`center=` is then ignored). Default is `1.0`.\n\n<code>[center]{.doc-parameter-name}[:]{.doc-parameter-annotation-sep} [tuple[float, float]]{.doc-parameter-annotation} [=]{.doc-parameter-default-sep .op} [(0.0, 0.0)]{.doc-parameter-default}</code>\n\n:   Center of the layout, as `(x, y)`. Default is the origin.\n\n<code>[attr_name]{.doc-parameter-name}[:]{.doc-parameter-annotation-sep} [Optional[str]]{.doc-parameter-annotation} [=]{.doc-parameter-default-sep .op} [None]{.doc-parameter-default}</code>\n\n:   Node attribute to store the positions in. If `None` (default), uses `\"pos\"`.\n\n<code>[return_dict]{.doc-parameter-name}[:]{.doc-parameter-annotation-sep} [bool]{.doc-parameter-annotation} [=]{.doc-parameter-default-sep .op} [[False]{.va}]{.doc-parameter-default}</code>\n\n:   If `True`, returns a dictionary instead of self. If `False` (default), stores the\n    positions on the nodes and returns self for chaining.\n:::\n\n## Returns {.doc-returns}\n\n::: {.doc-definition-items}\n<code> [Union[dict[int, tuple[float, float]], Graph]]{.doc-parameter-annotation}</code>\n\n:   If `return_dict=` is `True`: dictionary mapping node IDs to `(x, y)` tuples. If\n    `return_dict=` is `False` (default): returns self for method chaining.\n:::\n\n## Raises {.doc-raises}\n\n::: {.doc-definition-items}\n<code> [ValueError]{.doc-parameter-annotation}</code>\n\n:   If the graph is not planar (see `is_planar()`), or `scale=` or `center=` is\n    invalid.\n:::\n\n## See Also {.doc-see-also}\n\n[](`~check_planarity`) \n\n:   The planar embedding this layout draws.\n\n[](`~is_planar`) \n\n:   Whether a graph can be drawn without crossings at all.\n\n## Examples {.doc-examples}\n\n`K_4` is planar, so it can be drawn without crossings, with one node inside the triangle\nof the other three. `refine=False` and `scale=None` keep the grid coordinates:\n\n::: {#4156de15 .cell execution_count=1}\n``` {.python .cell-code}\nimport edgecase as ec\n\nec.Graph().add_full_graph(n=4).layout_planar(\n    refine=False, scale=None, return_dict=True\n)\n```\n\n::: {.cell-output .cell-output-display execution_count=1}\n```\n{1: (0.0, 0.0), 2: (4.0, 0.0), 3: (2.0, 1.0), 4: (2.0, 2.0)}\n```\n:::\n:::\n\n\nStored positions are what `plot(layout=...)` draws:\n\n::: {#377218fc .cell execution_count=2}\n``` {.python .cell-code}\nec.Graph().add_named_graph(\"dodecahedral\").layout_planar().plot(layout=\"pos\")\n```\n\n::: {.cell-output .cell-output-display execution_count=2}\n```{=html}\n<div class=\"ec-theme\" style=\"display:contents\"><style class=\"ec-theme-vars\">:where(.ec-theme){--ect-3239c383:#fafafa;--ect-e5dfd507:#94a3b8;--ect-8a2de1d4:#374151;--ect-57425a7e:rgba(15,23,42,0.88);-